      "text": "Instead, destruction is anticipated to be more practical when applied to treat small volume concentrate streams generated as byproducts of separation-based processes, including membrane reject concentrate streams generated by NF and RO treatment. To better represent full-scale treatment, pilot-scale assessments of membrane separation and destructive processes are necessary. To date, studies applying high-pressure membranes for PFAS treatment have been largely conducted using bench-scale flat sheet membrane systems ( Appleman et al., 2013 ) that may not be representative of treatment achieved at full-scale using spiral wound membranes due to changing conditions along the length of the element ( Fujioka et al., 2014 ). Simi - larly, UV-sulfite treatment of PFASs has been primarily limited to small volume ( < 1 L) bench-scale reactors treating PFASs spiked into deion - ized water matrices ( Bentel et al., 2020 ; Sun et al., 2018 ; Tenorio et al., 2020 ). Little is known about the degradation of PFASs in real contami - nated natural water matrices (e.g., groundwater) using reactors repre - sentative of full-scale UV treatment systems. Based on promising laboratory scale studies demonstrating the effective rejection and destruction of PFASs in AFFF impacted waters by"
